/** @page guarantees What Mosaic promises

  These are the promises a node, a writer and a reader can rely on. Each one is owned by a capability specification - the link
  in each heading is where the requirement lives, and that file wins if this page and the requirement ever disagree.

  @section writer Guarantees to a writer

  - **Your log is yours.** A log is identified by `(writer identity, channel)`; only the holder of that key can extend it. Nothing
    in the system can rewrite a record you signed - a replica either stores your chain or refuses to store anything.
  - **Sequence numbers are contiguous and never reused.** A restarted writer continues at head+1, so a reader can tell a gap from
    a rewrite.
  - **You can ask for retention.** With `min_replicas` set, records are kept until that many replicas confirm them, and an unmet
    floor is reported rather than hidden (`credit`, `retention`, `store/log-store`).

  @section follower Guarantees to a follower

  - **You converge.** Whatever the network does - loss, duplication, delay, reordering, a partition that later heals - a follower's
    log ends up identical to the writer's for every log it follows (`node/node-runtime`, `harness/simulation`).
  - **You never keep anything unverified.** Every record is checked for signature and chain before it is stored or shown to the
    application. A forged record is refused and counted (`log/replicated-log`).
  - **You can fall behind and catch up.** Repair is asked for, credit-paced and retried; a follower too far behind stops taking
    live records and fetches instead, so its memory is bounded (`flow/credit-control`).
  - **You can join while the writer is away.** Heads are available from any peer that holds the log (`node/node-runtime`).

  @section reader Guarantees to a reader

  - **History can be checked by anyone.** Signed, hash-chained records mean a holder of the head hash can verify the whole prefix
    without trusting who gave it to them (`core/record-integrity`).
  - **Two histories cannot be hidden from each other.** If a writer equivocates, a replica that sees both keeps both as evidence
    (`log/replicated-log`).
  - **A store can be verified offline.** `Mosaic verify` re-derives every hash and checks every signature and link; a gap, a broken
    link or an unreadable tail is a failure, never a short log (`cli/operations`).

  @section not Not promised

  - No ordering between different logs, no exactly-once execution, no consumer groups, no consensus.
  - The link is encrypted and the peer's key is pinned, but **data authority comes from signatures**, not from who carried the
    bytes: a link key never decides whether a record is true (`security`).
  - `0.x` minors may change APIs. The C ABI is not frozen yet.
*/

/** @page design Design at a glance

  Three roles, one class. A node is a writer for the logs it publishes, a replica for the patterns it follows, and a responder for
  anything it holds - there is no separate broker, store server or consumer process.

@verbatim
                publisher (writer)                        follower (replica)
        +-----------------------------+          +-----------------------------+
        |  LogWriter: seq, prev, sig  |          |  ReplicaLog: verify, store  |
        +--------------+--------------+          +--------------+--------------+
                       |                                        ^
        publish(channel, record)                               | record / head
                       |                                        |
                       v                                        |
                +----------------------------------------------+--+
                |                  Transport                      |
                |  live:  PUB/SUB, first frame = channel         |
                |  repair: ROUTER/DEALER by node id              |
                |  best effort; CURVE; peer key decided at ZAP   |
                +----------------------------------------------+--+
                       |                                        |
                       |  FETCH from any peer that holds it     |
                       +----------------------------------------+
@endverbatim

  @section log The log

  A writer owns one append-only log per channel. Each record carries a sequence number, a signed hash of its own contents and the
  hash of its predecessor - so a log is a chain, and any prefix can be verified from the head hash alone. Identity is the writer's
  public key, not a session id, so a restart keeps the log.

  @section repair Repair

  Live records are best effort; a follower that misses one notices the gap (or a head announcement) and asks for the range. Repair
  is credit-paced: the requester says how many records it can take, and each one returned spends a unit. Any peer holding the log
  can answer, not only the writer - which is what lets a writer go offline.

  @section channels Channels and patterns

  Channels are dotted names (`storage.eu.shard7`). A subscription is a pattern: literals, `*` for one segment, `>` for a trailing
  range. Matching happens locally on arrival, because a byte-prefix subscription cannot express "exactly one segment".

  @section credits Flow control

  The receiver grants credit; the sender spends it and never sends at zero. A follower that cannot keep up loses live records and
  recovers by repair rather than slowing the writer down. Queue limits are explicit, and overflow is a counted drop.

  @section jobs Jobs, optionally

  A job is an ordinary record on `svc.<name>`. Workers rank themselves deterministically for the job id and the top rank runs it,
  later ranks taking over if no result appears - so one worker dying delays a job instead of losing it.
*/
